                                                              "title":  "We ain\u0027t seen nothing yet.",
                                                              "source":  "Downtown Josh Brown",
                                                              "author":  "Josh Brown",
                                                              "url":  "https://www.downtownjoshbrown.com/p/we-ain-t-seen-nothing-yet",
                                                              "date":  "2026-08-02",
                                                              "imageUrl":  "https://media.beehiiv.com/cdn-cgi/image/fit=scale-down,quality=80,format=auto,onerror=redirect/uploads/asset/file/4ca57948-11e8-4308-b82e-eb095b528680/DSC02041.jpg?t=1785679292",
                                                              "summary":  "Josh Brown argues that today\u0027s AI compute infrastructure buildout is massively undersized for the agentic future ahead, where billions of autonomous AI assistants will operate continuously. The piece highlights a fundamental disagreement between Wall Street analysts worried about capex overbuilding and tech executives convinced demand from autonomous vehicles, robotics, and AI agents will dwarf current capacity. Brown sides firmly with the tech leaders, framing current infrastructure investment as the early phase of a decades-long buildout.",
                                                              "take":  "Brown\u0027s thesis is essentially a call to not fight the AI infrastructure tradeÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Âand historically, betting against the scale of infrastructure buildouts during technology platform transitions has been wrong. That said, the agentic demand wave he describes is still largely theoretical, and the gap between today\u0027s enterprise AI adoption rates and the billions-of-agents vision is enormous. The more useful read for enterprise leaders is that compute scarcity is coming: if Brown is even partially right, the organizations that secure long-term cloud infrastructure contracts and build AI capacity now will have a structural advantage over those who wait for the market to mature.",
                                                              "isNew":  true
