                                                                                "summary":  "Damodaran argues that the conventional wisdom linking higher interest rates to lower stock prices is an oversimplification that obscures how markets actually work. With US Treasury rates reaching 4.75% by August 2026, he shows that business cash flows adapt through revenue changes, margin adjustments, and reinvestment shifts ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Â meaning the rate-to-price transmission is never mechanical. US equities have continued to perform well despite rising rates globally because earnings growth has been strong enough to offset the higher discount rate headwind. The impact is highly uneven across sectors, with capital-light, high-pricing-power businesses largely immunized while rate-sensitive or margin-thin companies bear the brunt.",
                                                                                "take":  "Damodaran is doing what he does best: cutting through a market narrative that gets repeated so often it calcifies into dogma. The \u0027rates up = stocks down\u0027 reflex drives a lot of amateur portfolio decisions and even some professional ones, yet the empirical record is far messier. The practical implication for investors is not to reflexively de-risk on rate moves, but to drill into which specific businesses have the cash-flow flexibility to absorb higher discount rates ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Â and to recognize that in a strong earnings-growth environment, the denominator story (rates) can easily be overwhelmed by the numerator story (earnings). The more useful framework is not the level of rates but the direction of surprises relative to what equity multiples already assume.",
                                                                                "isNew":  true
