                                                                                "summary":  "Damodaran critiques venture capital\u0027s ingrained bias toward growth-at-any-cost, arguing that the rush to scale has become structurally detached from the question of whether a business is actually building sustainable value. He maps out how public equity investors flooding into private markets ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Â and the resulting pressure on founders to show ever-larger top-line numbers before an exit ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Â has turbo-charged the problem. The result is a cohort of companies that go public at enormous valuations with no credible path to profitability, disrupting incumbent industries while destroying value in the process. He warns that this dynamic does not just hurt VC fund returns; it creates macro-level misallocations when capital follows scale metrics rather than value creation.",
                                                                                "take":  "This piece is Damodaran at his most pointed, and the critique lands. The VC-scale model has a self-reinforcing logic: LPs reward funds that generate markups, funds reward founders who generate revenue multiples, and nobody in the chain is strongly incentivized to ask \u0027but is this a real business?\u0027 The AI wave is arguably rerunning this playbook at an even larger dollar scale. The actionable lens for public-market investors is skepticism toward any AI-adjacent IPO or SPAC that leads with user growth or GMV without showing a plausible unit-economics story ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Â the pattern Damodaran describes predicts exactly where the bodies will be found in 3-5 years.",
