                                                                                "imageUrl":  "https://media.beehiiv.com/cdn-cgi/image/fit=scale-down,quality=80,format=auto,onerror=redirect/uploads/asset/file/4ca57948-11e8-4308-b82e-eb095b528680/DSC02041.jpg?t=1785679292",
                                                                                "summary":  "Brown\u0027s core argument is that the data center overbuilding narrative fundamentally misunderstands the demand side of the equation: when a billion people each have a personal AI assistant running autonomously, plus autonomous vehicles, robots, and agentic workflows, today\u0027s compute infrastructure will look laughably undersized. He contends that the tech executives committing trillion-dollar capex budgets are making a rational forward bet, not a speculative one, while critics anchored to current AI usage patterns are extrapolating from an early and unrepresentative baseline. The piece draws an analogy to skeptics of early internet infrastructure spending who failed to anticipate what \u0027everyone on the internet\u0027 would actually require.",
                                                                                "take":  "Brown is making a genuine bull case, not just cheerleading ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Â the argument that today\u0027s critics are anchoring on 2024-era AI usage to judge 2030-era demand requirements is intellectually sound. The risk he does not fully engage with is the timing mismatch: even if the demand eventually arrives, the capex is being incurred now, and the depreciation burden will hit earnings before the revenue materializes. History (fiber optic overbuild circa 2000) shows that being right about long-run demand is cold comfort if the capital structure can\u0027t survive the gap. The smarter read may be that hyperscalers with fortress balance sheets will absorb the timing risk fine, while the infrastructure picks-and-shovels plays face more asymmetric risk.",
