                                                                                "date":  "2026-09-29",
                                                                                "imageUrl":  null,
                                                                                "summary":  "Santoli argues that years of equity outperformance have conditioned investors to abandon diversification, but rising yields have now created the most attractive bond entry point in decades. With the 30-year Treasury yield scaling to its highest level since 2002, bonds now offer enough yield cushion to absorb further rate upside while providing genuine portfolio insurance against an equity drawdown. He notes that investor sentiment toward bonds has curdled into \u0027fear and disgust,\u0027 which is historically a contrarian setup. The piece frames year-end rebalancing as a potential catalyst for bond demand to reassert itself as institutions mechanically shift back toward fixed-income allocations.",
                                                                                "take":  "The timing of this piece is notable: it lands exactly as yields hit multi-decade highs and equity valuations remain stretched on a forward PE basis, which is precisely the risk-reward flip the traditional 60/40 framework was designed to exploit. Santoli is not calling a top in equities, but he is making a subtle, important point: the asymmetry has shifted. Bonds now offer a non-trivial real yield and negative equity-beta protection, whereas 18 months ago they offered neither. For investors running overweight equity portfolios built on the TINA logic of the low-rate era, this piece is a nudge to revisit whether that logic still holds ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Â and the honest answer is that it mostly does not.",
                                                                                "isNew":  true
